  "model_card": {
    "summary": "Final completed FP32/BF16/FP16-autocast training comparison TinyLMs; stored deployment parameters remain FP32.",
    "scope": "同FP32起始權重、資料、batch及更新嘗試數；AMP依算子選dtype，權重與Adam狀態保留FP32，FP16在CUDA使用GradScaler。",
    "limitations": [
      "Single seed, short training and small fixed datasets do not establish universal architecture or precision superiority.",
      "Autocast chooses operator dtypes. Stored weights/Adam state remain FP32; FP16 scaler skips and successful update counts must be read separately. All three branches completed 200 successful updates from 200 attempts, with zero skips and 22493 supervised targets. FP16 uses CUDA GradScaler; its logged scale snapshots remain 65536.0. This run demonstrates no overflow-recovery event.",
      "Each branch copies the same original synthetic SFT weights. Reported held-out quality and inference timing use that branch's autocast context; the provided CPU inference command loads the exported FP32 parameters without reproducing the CUDA autocast timing or numerical path.",
      "On this small run, both AMP branches have slower measured warm training steps and forward medians than FP32. Test exact match is FP32 7/10, BF16 6/10 and FP16 4/10; lower precision does not guarantee faster execution or equal task quality.",
      "fp32.pt is the predeclared reference, not selected by minimum held-out NLL: BF16 has lower test NLL (0.3270883421966995 versus FP32 0.36271562438080274) despite fewer exact matches."
    ],
